2186045417] [17592186508878 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508878 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508878 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508892 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508892 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508892 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508892 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508577 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508892 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7dafff5-b3a8-518e-8b51-5bc2eaf0b1e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508905 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508905 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508905 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508905 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508905 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6880c81-e1e8-5f43-89ee-3d61afafa579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344853" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9e/9ea7b23d8dd14a0ed092152592800022400ee73e7b371146f5e972900f2c2f29.png)\n\nA community organizer wants to install a border around a walkway made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. She has only $120\\,m$ of border material. The organizer wishes to maximize the area of the **rectangle**. Find $x+2r$ where $x$ and $2r$ are the dimensions of the rectangle that has the greatest area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x+2r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508912 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0e/0ef7a2645946099b10772fdc3fee46c582ad43debb0cb53041c46aa99c203ab1.png)\n\nSofia is designing a park that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the park must be $16\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Sofia wants a park with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508921 #edb/ref 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $16\\text{ km},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n16=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =16-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{32}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/60e04faa7448d351706ee1af70a8620590ca16e0debce7dd2e58631462594132.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $DEF$ with area $A=144$ square units. Determine the smallest possible perimeter $P$ of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508928 #edb/ref 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $144$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n144={r}^{2}\\frac{\\theta }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{288}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =288 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =144 \\\\ r & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{576}{{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=12$ is a minimum.\n\nFinally, when $r=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{288}{144} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter are $r=12$ and $\\theta =2.$ Therefore, the smallest perimeter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12+12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/59/59949e4ba7818ea08d4613fee703a2c973ffa4953c820f04c1ae9279147dad56.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=180$ units. What is the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508930 #edb/ref 1074 "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $180$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n180=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{180-2r}{r}=\\frac{180}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{180}{r}-2) \\\\  & =90r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=90-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 90-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=45$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(45)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(45)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=45$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344791" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5cd71fa90a1213292961960facf049a1333beb63abeb53f08ba18173281fb330.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $ABC$ with perimeter $P=80$ units. Determine the largest possible area of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe maximum area is\n{{field-1}}\nsquare units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508940 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $80$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n80=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{80-2r}{r}=\\frac{80}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{80}{r}-2) \\\\  & =40r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=40-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 40-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=20$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(20)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(20)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=20$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $20,$ and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =40r-{r}^{2} \\\\  & =40(20)-(20)^{2} \\\\  & =400.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508892 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d21626823e213a4c2d198f42e8e66d353364e722c1add41b35fcbc138ed8bff4.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $JKL$ with area $A=121$ square units. Determine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the smallest possible perimeter. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508946 #edb/ref 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $121$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n121=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}121 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{242}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =242 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =121 \\\\ r & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=11$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{484}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=11$ is a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter is $r=11$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f1/f17ce467f5bff34f0c04326d1a23ca8621f459571be8c98a745174a3d5587337.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=100$ units. Determine the angle $\\theta $ (in radians) of the circular sector with the largest possible area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}\nradians" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508955 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $100$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{100-2r}{r}=\\frac{100}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{100}{r}-2) \\\\  & =50r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=50-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 50-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=25$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(25)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(25)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=25$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=25$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{100}{r}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{25}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15089" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c9/c9b79e710dda94e6c4d91a157317977f5b2388202bb63b08fcc325ea93f0453c.png)\n\nA gardener wants to create a flowerbed in the shape of a rectangle topped by a semicircle, as shown in the diagram. He needs the flowerbed to have an area of $\\frac{25}{9}\\,{cm}^{2}.$ What is the radius $r$ of the flowerbed with the smallest possible perimeter?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508956 #edb/ref 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $x$ the side of the rectangle.\nThen, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=\\pi r+2x+2r.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $\\frac{25}{9}$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}=2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{9} & =2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2rx & =\\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\pi r+2x+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+2(\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4})+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+\\frac{25}{9r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)r+\\frac{25}{9r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 9{r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{50}{9(\\pi +4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{50}{9{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/be/be50f6313cb20e06c3d850a3e81f267ac5e2cdc5c2a308280ba6367c08d66fec.png)\n\nMatt is planning a field whose surface is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the field must be $9\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Matt wants a field with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508969 #edb/ref 1074 "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $9$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =9-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{18}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-17957" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0d/0da2ed0bca11725e7ffb3fa3c5952807e406530a0625b01ebb476f0145b64e30.png)\n\nA farmer wants to fence a field that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. He has only $200\\,m$ of fencing. What is the length $2x$ of the side of the field that covers the greatest area?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508984 #edb/ref 1074 "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
